## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests advanced bit manipulation skills. A palindrome check requires full 128-bit reversal and a single branchless comparison. It assesses understanding of endianness, cross-lane bit operations, and how to compose a complex operation from a reusable primitive.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit bit reversal (SWAR-style steps)
- Branchless comparison via bitwise XOR
- Endian-agnostic bit ordering across two 64-bit lanes

## Code Example
```c
int isBitPalindrome128(uint128 x) {
    uint128 r = reverseBits128(x);
    uint128 d = x ^ r;
    return (d == 0) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement a branchless popcount for 128-bit data using only bitwise ops, a core skill in performance-critical code paths like fingerprinting or anomaly detection.

## Key Concepts

- Branchless bit counting (SWAR)
- Apply per-64-bit halves independently
- Combine results without overflow or branches

## Code Example

```javascript
// Conceptual: 64-bit helper (C-like pseudocode)
unsigned int popcount64(unsigned long long x){
  x -= (x>>1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x>>2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (unsigned int)((x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56);
}
unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x){
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt if you needed to accumulate counts in a streaming fashion?
- How does this interact with endianness in cross-language bindings?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about cross-boundary bit interactions and implement constant-time checks using only bitwise ops. 

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise shifts and masks across multiword values
- Detecting adjacent bits within each 64-bit half and across the 64-bit boundary
- Branchless boolean results for interview clarity

## Code Example
```javascript
int hasAdjacentOnes128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t a = x.lo & (x.lo << 1);
  uint64_t b = x.hi & (x.hi << 1);
  int cross = ((x.lo >> 63) & 1) & (x.hi & 1);
  return (a | b | cross) != 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to detect a run of exactly k adjacent ones? 
- How would you implement a similar check for 256-bit values represented as two uint128s?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses branch-free 128-bit rotation, a common primitive in crypto and low-level optimizations.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values via two 64-bit halves
- Branchless selection with masks
- Cross-half rotation logic and edge cases (n=0, n=64, n=127)

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, uint8_t n) {
  uint64_t n0 = n & 63;
  uint64_t m  = -((int)((n >> 6) & 1)); // 0 or -1
  uint64_t hi1 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo1 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t hi2 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo2 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = (hi1 & ~m) | (hi2 & m);
  r.lo = (lo1 & ~m) | (lo2 & m);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How might you test for constant-time properties across inputs?
- How would you extend to a constant-time generic rotate for arbitrary bit-widths?

## Why This Is Asked

