## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reduce a 128-bit input to a single bit using only shifts and XORs, a common low-level optimization pattern. It emphasizes branchless, predictable timing.

## Key Concepts
- XOR folding to accumulate parity across halves
- Branchless, constant-time bitwise manipulation
- Handling 128-bit input via hi/lo halves without loops

## Code Example
```javascript
function parity128(hi, lo) {
  let p = hi ^ lo;
  p = p ^ (p >>> 32);
  p = p ^ (p >>> 16);
  p = p ^ (p >>> 8);
  p = p ^ (p >>> 4);
  p = p ^ (p >>> 2);
  p = p ^ (p >>> 1);
  return p & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 256-bit values?
- What's the performance impact of using XOR vs. addition for parity?
- Can you implement this using only addition and subtraction?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests compact, deterministic bit-trickery without loops, showing knowledge of parallel popcount progressions.

## Key Concepts
- Masks for pairwise sums
- Shifts to propagate counts
- Edge-case: 0 yields 0

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t x) {
  x = (x & 0x55555555) + ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F);
  x = (x & 0x00FF00FF) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF);
  x = (x & 0x0000FFFF) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF);
  return x & 0x3F;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 64-bit inputs without 128-bit math?
- How to test exhaustively across 32-bit space?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to reason about cross-half bit patterns and leverage a reversal primitive to detect palindromicity without loops.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit representations split across two 64-bit halves
- Cross-half bit equality, palindrome concept
- Using an existing reversal primitive to avoid per-bit work

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
int isPalindrome128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t hi_rev, lo_rev;
  reverse128(hi, lo, &hi_rev, &lo_rev);
  return (hi == hi_rev && lo == lo_rev) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- Could you implement a pure bitwise variant that does not rely on reverse128?
- How would you generalize to 256-bit values with four halves?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to compare 128-bit values split across lanes and derive a scalar metric without 128-bit math.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit data split into hi/lo lanes
- XOR to highlight differences
- 64-bit popcount for bit counting

## Code Example

```javascript
// Reference approach in C-like pseudocode; interview would use C/C++
int hammingDistance128(uint64_t a_hi, uint64_t a_lo, uint64_t b_hi, uint64_t b_lo) {
  uint64_t dh = a_hi ^ b_hi;
  uint64_t dl = a_lo ^ b_lo;
  return __builtin_popcountll(dh) + __builtin_popcountll(dl);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to implement popcount without builtins?
- What if values are stored in different endianness?
- How would this extend to 256-bit values?

