questions.explanation:
## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about multi-word bit manipulations and safe 64→128-bit extension without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit reversal via two 64-bit reversals and swapping halves
- Parallel-bits reversal (mask/shifts)
- Endianness-insensitive mapping of bit indices

## Code Example
```javascript
function reverse64(x) {
  x = (x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n | (x & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n;
  x = (x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n | (x & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n;
  x = (x >> 4n) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) << 4n;
  x = (x >> 8n) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) << 8n;
  x = (x >> 16n) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) << 16n;
  x = (x >> 32n) | (x << 32n);
  return x;
}
function reverse128(hi, lo) {
  const revLo = reverse64(lo);
  const revHi = reverse64(hi);
  return { hi: revLo, lo: revHi };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to streaming 128-bit values?
- How to verify correctness with random tests?

## Why This Is Asked
This asks for deep bit-manipulation skill: a palindrome across 128 bits is a nontrivial symmetry that can be checked in constant time with a reversal network, not with loops. It reveals understanding of endian-ness and 128-bit layout.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit word as {uint64 hi, lo}
- Bit-reversal networks using masks: 0x5555..., 0x3333..., 0x0F0F..., etc.
- Palindrome property across all 128 bits
- Edge-case tests (all zeros, ends-set pattern)

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
  x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
  x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
  x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
  x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
  x = (x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL | (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32;
  return x;
}
static inline uint128 reverse128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r; r.lo = reverse64(x.hi); r.hi = reverse64(x.lo); return r;
}
int isPalindromicBits128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r = reverse128(x);
  return (x.hi == r.hi) && (x.lo == r.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support streaming 128-bit palindromicity with partial data?
- What are the performance implications on 64-bit vs 128-bit targets?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to implement a constant-time bit reversal to verify a binary palindrome using only bitwise ops.

## Key Concepts

- Palindrome property in binary
- Bit-reversal networks with masks
- Branch-free comparison

## Code Example

```javascript
// C-like implementation (illustrative)
int isBinaryPalindrome32(uint32_t n) {
  uint32_t x = n;
  x = ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555) | ((x & 0x55555555) << 1);
  x = ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333) | ((x & 0x33333333) << 2);
  x = ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F) | ((x & 0x0F0F0F0F) << 4);
  x = ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF) | ((x & 0x00FF00FF) << 8);
  x = (x >> 16) | (x << 16);
  return (x == n);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

